WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:08.950
Forty-two years after unbreakable encryption tools were first conceived

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:11.240
that allowed individuals to communicate in private,

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:13.240
they’ve finally gone fully mainstream.

00:00:13.240 --> 00:00:15.780
“What’sApp has introduced a new level
of encryption.”

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.560
“Amazon’s Fire OS5 will offer full disc
encryption.”

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:22.880
“So encrypted, privacy protected, disappearing”

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.700
One milestone came in 2016,

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.980
when the world’s largest messaging service
announced it would offer default end to end encryption

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.320
on all communications.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.360
“What’sApp messages can now only be read
by their intended recipients

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.520
and can’t be accessed by anyone else including
governments or What’sApp staff.”

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.440
Law enforcement and intelligence agencies
are still reckoning with this new reality.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.980
For decades they demanded tech companies hand
over private data on their users,

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:48.720
sometimes without obtaining warrants.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.960
So companies like Apple have changed their
policies,

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:55.100
making it so that individual users are the
only ones with the keys to their own data.

00:00:56.020 --> 00:00:59.240
“Sayed Farook and and his wife Tashfeen
Malik opened fire.”

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:02.620
“Hours later they were in turn killed by
police...”

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.060
This new era of consumer privacy led to a
standoff in 2016,

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:09.790
when the FBI demanded access to an encrypted
iPhone

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.900
belonging to a terrorist from San Bernardino,
California.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.060
The FBI wanted Apple to write software that
would weaken the iPhone’s built-in security.

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.960
Apple refused, telling authorities if the
company were to create such flawed software

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.820
that would work like a backdoor,

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.320
it would create opportunities for hackers
to steal private information.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:32.000
What is at stake here is: Can the government
compel Apple to write software

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.800
that we believe would make hundreds of millions
of customers venerable around the world.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:40.500
It is in our view the software equivalent
of cancer.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:45.320
And yet there's plenty of consumer data that's
unencrypted and accessible by large tech companies.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:49.560
From Facebook to Gmail, law enforcement can
still get a warrant to get a hold of your data.

